      "text": "Yes. In Kotlin, the best way to do this is to create a **Class** or an **Object** that holds the variables (like glucose, ATP, etc.).\n\nHere are the two best ways to do it.\n\n### Option 1: The \"Class\" Approach (Recommended)\nThis is the best approach for App development. You create a class that holds the state. Inside your Composable, you create an instance of that class using `remember`. This ensures that if you close and reopen the app (or the screen), the simulation resets cleanly.\n\n```kotlin\npackage jon.sharp.metabolism.simulator\n\nimport androidx.compose.foundation.layout.Column\nimport androidx.compose.material3.Button\nimport androidx.compose.material3.Text\nimport androidx.compose.runtime.*\nimport kotlinx.coroutines.delay\n\n// 1. Define the Shared Object (The Model)\nclass MetabolismEngine {\n    // These variables are shared state\n    var glucose: Int \u003d 100\n    var insulin: Int \u003d 10\n    var energy: Int \u003d 0\n\n    // Logic function\n    fun runSimulation() {\n        if (glucose \u003e 0) {\n            glucose -\u003d 5\n            energy +\u003d 2\n            insulin +\u003d 1\n        }\n    }\n\n    // Display function\n    fun getDataToDisplay(): String {\n        return \"Glucose: $glucose | ATP: $energy | Insulin: $insulin\"\n    }\n}\n\n@Composable\nfun App() {\n    // 2. Create the instance. \u0027remember\u0027 keeps this object alive \n    // as long as the UI is on screen.\n    val engine \u003d remember { MetabolismEngine() }\n    \n    var isSimulating by remember { mutableStateOf(false) }\n    var displayText by remember { mutableStateOf(engine.getDataToDisplay()) }\n\n    LaunchedEffect(isSimulating) {\n        if (isSimulating) {\n            while (true) {\n                // 3. Both functions now run on the SAME \u0027engine\u0027 instance\n                engine.runSimulation() \n                displayText \u003d engine.getDataToDisplay()\n                delay(1000L)\n            }\n        }