      "text": "Based on your previous code, there are two main places you usually want to launch a coroutine:\n\n### 1. Inside a Composable (e.g., On Button Click)\nYou cannot call `suspend` functions (like `delay`) directly inside an `onClick` lambda. You need `rememberCoroutineScope`.\n\n```kotlin\n@Composable\nfun App() {\n    // 1. Create the scope tied to this UI\u0027s lifecycle\n    val scope \u003d rememberCoroutineScope() \n\n    Button(onClick \u003d {\n        // 2. Launch the coroutine here\n        scope.launch {\n            println(\"Coroutine started\")\n            delay(1000) // Now allowed\n            println(\"Coroutine finished\")\n        }\n    }) {\n        Text(\"Click me\")\n    }\n}\n```\n\n### 2. Inside a Class (e.g., Your SimulationEngine)\nIf you want the `SimulationEngine` to handle the background work internally (instead of the UI `LaunchedEffect` driving it), you need to create a `CoroutineScope`.\n\n```kotlin\nimport kotlinx.coroutines.*\n\nclass SimulationEngine {\n    // 1. Define a scope (IO is good for calculation/background work)\n    private val scope \u003d CoroutineScope(Dispatchers.IO) \n    \n    fun startBackgroundSimulation() {\n        // 2. Launch off the scope\n        scope.launch {\n            while(true) {\n                // Heavy calculation here\n                delay(1000)\n            }\n        }\n    }\n\n    // Don\u0027t forget to cleanup if the engine is destroyed!\n    fun stop() {\n        scope.cancel()\n    }\n}\n```\n\n### Summary of which to use:\n\n| Scenario | Solution |\n| :--- | :--- |\n| **I want a loop to run automatically when the screen opens** | Use `LaunchedEffect(Unit)` |\n| **I want to run code when the user clicks a button** | Use `rememberCoroutineScope().launch {}` |\n| **I want to run code inside a plain Kotlin class** | Create a `CoroutineScope(Dispatchers.IO).launch {}` |\n\n### Dispatchers Cheat Sheet\nWhen you launch, you usually want to specify *where* it runs if you are creating the scope manually:\n*   `Dispatchers.Main`: Updates the UI.\n*   `Dispatchers.IO`: Database, Network, or simple background tasks.\n*   `Dispatchers.Default`: Heavy math or CPU-intensive algorithms (like your Metabolism logic).",
